# A simple backward rule
#
# Is five more interesting than three? A backward rule defines "more
# interesting" as "greater than", so the question is answered on demand by a
# numeric comparison. A forward rule asks it and records the answer. The
# example shows how a backward rule (<=) and a forward rule (=>) work
# together.
#
# See <https://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/doc/tutorial-1.pdf>

@prefix math: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/math#> .
@prefix log: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/log#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/#> .

# Backward rule: X is more interesting than Y when X is greater than Y. It is
# used only when some other rule asks a :moreInterestingThan question.
{
    ?X :moreInterestingThan ?Y .
} <= {
    ?X math:greaterThan ?Y .
} .

# Forward rule: ask whether 5 is more interesting than 3, and record it.
{
    5 :moreInterestingThan 3 .
} => {
    5 :isIndeedMoreInterestingThan 3 .
} .
